---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["how-time-cards-work"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    A time card is a record of when you started and stopped work each day in a
    pay period. Payroll uses it to decide how many hours to pay you for,
    including overtime. It can be a paper sheet, a spreadsheet, a punch-clock
    card, or entries in a time-tracking app — the principle is the same.
  </p>

  <h2>What a time card records</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The <strong>date</strong> and <strong>day of the week</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Clock in</strong> and <strong>clock out</strong> times — one pair per shift, or more if you leave and return.</li>
    <li><strong>Unpaid breaks</strong>, usually the meal break, in minutes.</li>
    <li>Sometimes a <strong>job, project, or department code</strong> so hours can be allocated to the right budget.</li>
    <li>A <strong>total</strong> per day and per period, and often a space for your signature and a supervisor's approval.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Paper, punch clock, or app</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>How time is captured</th><th>Watch for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Paper / spreadsheet</td><td>You write the times</td><td>Estimating from memory; math errors in the totals</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mechanical punch clock</td><td>Stamps a card</td><td>Rounding of the stamped time</td></tr>
      <tr><td>App or web portal</td><td>Button press, sometimes GPS-tagged</td><td>Auto-deducted meal breaks you did not take</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Biometric (fingerprint / face)</td><td>Scan on a shared terminal</td><td>Queue time before your shift; scanner misreads</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Filling one out</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Record your actual start and finish times to the minute, as they happen — not from memory at the end of the week.</li>
    <li>Note the length of any unpaid break. If you worked through it, record that you did and do not deduct it.</li>
    <li>At the end of each day, work out the hours: <code>(clock out &minus; clock in) &minus; unpaid break</code>.</li>
    <li>At the end of the period, add the daily totals and convert to decimal hours for payroll.</li>
    <li>Sign it if a signature is required, submit it by the deadline, and keep your own copy.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The <a href="/calculators/time-card-calculator/">Time Card Calculator</a> does
    steps 3&ndash;4 for a whole week and applies the overtime split. For a stack
    of raw in/out punches, the
    <a href="/calculators/time-clock-calculator/">Time Clock Calculator</a>
    totals them into decimal hours.
  </p>

  <h2>Pay periods and the workweek</h2>
  <p>
    A time card covers one pay period — commonly weekly (52 a year), biweekly
    (26), or semimonthly (24, on set dates). Overtime is calculated on each
    <em>workweek</em>, a fixed seven-day span the employer defines, even when
    the pay period is longer or splits a week. See
    <a href="/guides/what-is-a-pay-period/">what is a pay period</a> and
    <a href="/guides/biweekly-vs-semimonthly-pay/">biweekly vs semimonthly
    pay</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How employers round punch times</h2>
  <p>
    Rounding each punch to the nearest 5, 6 (a tenth of an hour), or 15 minutes
    is common. In the US the FLSA allows it only if, over time, it does not
    systematically underpay employees, and it must be applied the same way
    whether it helps or hurts the worker. The details and the "7-minute rule"
    are in <a href="/guides/time-clock-rounding-rules/">time clock rounding
    rules</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections and missed punches</h2>
  <p>
    If you forget to clock in or out, tell your supervisor the same day and
    submit a written correction with the actual times. Do not leave it blank or
    guess later. Employers can edit time records, but they must reflect the
    hours you actually worked; an edit that removes real worked time is a
    violation.
  </p>

  <h2>Record-keeping requirements</h2>
  <p>
    US employers must keep accurate records of hours worked for non-exempt
    employees. Basic payroll records are kept for at least three years, and the
    records used to compute pay (time cards, schedules, wage-rate tables) for at
    least two. Your own copy of each time card is your evidence if there is ever
    a dispute about hours or pay.
  </p>

  <h2>Do salaried employees fill out time cards?</h2>
  <p>
    Exempt salaried employees are not legally required to track hours for pay,
    but many still submit time records for project costing, leave tracking, or
    client billing. Salaried <em>non-exempt</em> employees should track hours,
    because they are still owed overtime — see
    <a href="/guides/exempt-vs-non-exempt-employees/">exempt vs non-exempt
    employees</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes that cost hours</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Logging <em>scheduled</em> hours instead of <em>actual</em> hours worked.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that an overnight shift crosses midnight.</li>
    <li>Letting the system auto-deduct a lunch that was not actually taken.</li>
    <li>Writing 7 hours 30 minutes as "7.30" instead of 7.50.</li>
    <li>Not recording short shifts, call-backs, or required tasks done before or after clocking.</li>
    <li>Missing the submission deadline and being paid an estimate instead.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>What if my employer changed my time card?</h3>
  <p>
    Ask for the reason in writing and compare it against your own record.
    Legitimate corrections happen; removing worked time does not. Persistent
    discrepancies can be reported to the Department of Labor or your state
    labour agency.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I be disciplined for a time-card error?</h3>
  <p>
    For an honest mistake, usually not. Deliberately falsifying a time card —
    yours or a coworker's ("buddy punching") — is grounds for termination.
  </p>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/21-flsa-recordkeeping" rel="noopener">US Department of Labor — Fact Sheet #21: Recordkeeping Requirements under the FLSA</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/22-flsa-hours-worked" rel="noopener">US Department of Labor — Fact Sheet #22: Hours Worked</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/subtitle-B/chapter-V/subchapter-B/part-785/section-785.48" rel="noopener">29 CFR 785.48 — Use of time clocks</a></li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
